    "description": "Iran's parliament scrutinizes government through investigations, questions, confidence procedures, complaints, and financial auditing. These constitutional powers establish accountability mechanisms, but their existence alone does not demonstrate consistent enforcement or effective remedies. [s1]",
    "tags": ["Parliament", "Accountability", "Public finance", "Executive scrutiny"],
    "status": null,
    "editorial_status": "needs_research",
    "as_of_date": "2026-10-26",
    "created_at": "2026-10-27T23:26:33Z",
    "updated_at": "2026-10-27T23:26:33Z",
    "seo": {
      "title": "Parliamentary Oversight in Iran | Iran, Unfolded",
      "description": "How Iran's parliament scrutinizes ministers and public spending, with distinctions between constitutional powers and demonstrated outcomes."
    },
    "content": [
      {
        "id": "constitutional-tools",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Different tools for different questions",
        "content": "The Majles, Iran's national parliament, has a general investigative power under Article 76. Article 88 provides for questioning ministers and the president; Article 89 governs interpellation and confidence consequences. Article 90 provides a parliamentary channel for written complaints about the work of the legislature, executive, or judiciary. [s1]\n\nFinancial scrutiny has a separate institutional basis. Articles 54–55 place the Supreme Audit Court under parliamentary supervision and require examination of bodies using the national budget. [s1]",
        "source_ids": ["s1"]
      },
      {
        "id": "dismissal-example",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "A documented consequence: ministerial removal",
        "content": "On 2 March 2025, parliament removed Economy Minister Abdolnaser Hemmati through a no-confidence vote. Reuters reported that the proceedings concerned allegations of economic mismanagement and the falling national currency. The episode demonstrates that parliamentary scrutiny can end a minister's tenure. It does not establish that the minister alone caused the economic conditions cited against him. [s2]\n\nThis distinction matters when reading coverage of oversight. A dismissal is an observable institutional outcome; whether dismissal improves economic management is a separate question requiring subsequent evidence. Likewise, lawmakers' accusations should be attributed as accusations rather than adopted as independently established findings. [s2]",
        "source_ids": ["s2"]
      },
      {
        "id": "reading-oversight",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "How to assess an oversight claim",
        "content": "A useful assessment follows the record from the original question or complaint to the response and any resulting action. The constitutional text establishes available procedures, while the Hemmati case supplies evidence of a particular consequence. Neither source provides a comprehensive measure of how frequently scrutiny produces corrections, financial recovery, or changes in administrative practice. [s1][s2]\n\nReaders should therefore distinguish an investigation being announced, findings being published, responsibility being assigned, and a remedy being implemented. Those are separate evidentiary stages. This page does not treat a public confrontation or a change of personnel as proof that the underlying problem was resolved.",
        "source_ids": ["s1", "s2"]
      }
    ],
    "datasets": [],
