    "description": "Iran's executive institutions include the presidency, ministries, and specialized administrative organizations. Understanding their work requires distinguishing political leadership, sectoral administration, planning, and the responsibilities assigned to affiliated bodies. [s1][s2]",
    "tags": ["Executive government", "Public administration", "Ministries", "Planning", "Institutional coordination"],
    "status": null,
    "editorial_status": "needs_research",
    "as_of_date": "2026-10-26",
    "created_at": "2026-10-27T23:26:33Z",
    "updated_at": "2026-10-27T23:26:33Z",
    "seo": {
      "title": "Executive Institutions in Iran | Iran, Unfolded",
      "description": "An introduction to Iran's executive institutions, distinguishing ministries, planning bodies, affiliated organizations, and shared responsibilities."
    },
    "content": [
      {
        "id": "executive-framework",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Leadership and administration",
        "content": "The Constitution assigns executive functions to the president and ministers except where authority is reserved to the Leadership. Ministers require parliamentary confidence, and the president chairs the Council of Ministers. The president also has constitutional responsibility for planning, budgeting, and administrative affairs, with provision for delegation. [s1]\n\nThis framework makes the cabinet central to administration without making it the sole location of executive authority. An institutional guide must therefore distinguish cabinet responsibilities from powers assigned elsewhere. [s1]",
        "source_ids": ["s1"]
      },
      {
        "id": "coordination-example",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Why one policy can involve several institutions",
        "content": "A project-financing example illustrates the practical importance of institutional boundaries. In its February 2017 study of potential Japanese development lending to Iran, the Japan International Cooperation Agency, or JICA, distinguished the planning organization's budget and project-review role from the finance ministry's affiliated investment organization, which handled financing coordination. It separately identified roles for the foreign ministry and central bank. [s2]\n\nThis was an operational assessment of a particular financing context, not a universal organization chart. Nevertheless, it shows why a ministry's involvement should not be interpreted as exclusive control. Project preparation, financing negotiations, legal assurances, and implementation can involve different public bodies. The study describes arrangements at the time of its research; it cannot by itself verify their continuation in 2026. [s2]",
        "source_ids": ["s2"]
      },
      {
        "id": "specialized-administration",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Affiliated organizations matter",
        "content": "The finance portfolio offers another useful distinction. The Asian Productivity Organization's 2016 study examines the Iranian National Tax Administration as a specialized tax-collection institution and discusses its organizational development and performance measurement. This places attention on the administrative body delivering a function rather than only on the minister's office. [s3]\n\nFor readers exploring executive institutions, the useful questions are concrete: which body sets policy, which administers it, what evidence records delivery, and what period does that evidence cover? An organizational description explains responsibility. A performance assessment additionally needs defined outputs, comparable observations, and an account of limitations. The productivity study is historical evidence of such an assessment, not a current rating of Iranian administration. [s3]",
        "source_ids": ["s3"]
      },
      {
        "id": "using-directory",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Reading the institutional directory",
        "content": "The pages beneath this category separate economic affairs and finance, industry and trade, communications technology, roads and urban development, science and higher education, and planning and budgeting. Their purpose is to help readers identify the institution relevant to a subject before tracing the applicable mandate and implementation record.\n\nA complete account still requires current mandates and organizational records for each portfolio. This draft establishes a framework for reading those institutions and a documented coordination example; it does not yet provide a verified comparative account of every branch listed in the directory.",
        "source_ids": []
      }
    ],
